  number={},
  pages={1-6},
  abstract={Many electronic loads in DC distribution systems behave as constant-power loads (CPLs) which are known to exhibit negative incremental impedance and have destabilizing effect in the system. To improve the stability of DC systems with CPLs, research efforts have been focused on impedance-based stability criterion, design of advanced controllers, and passive or active damping solutions. In this paper, an adaptive method based on active damping is proposed. Therein, the load bus voltage is monitored and an active damper is used to stabilize the system once an instability is detected. The proposed methodology is less conservative than traditional methods and its damping characteristics are established “on-the-fly” in real-time to achieve effective stabilization under various CPLs and different operating conditions that may be unknown a priori. The methodology is verified on a 48/24 VDC telecom system consisting of commercially available converters.},
